hypotheses.statement:
Submultiplicativity defines $$\label{eq:rho}
 \rho=\lim_{n\to\infty}\|L^n\|^{1/n},
 \qquad \beta=\log_2\rho,\qquad 0\le\beta\le1.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
There exist constants $0<\varepsilon<1/4$ and $C<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:uniform}
 M_{-1}[f'](r)\le C(1-r)^{-1/4+\varepsilon}
 \qquad\text{for every }f\in\mathcal S\text{ and every }1/2\le r<1.$$ Consequently $B_b(-1)<1/4$, and Kraetzer’s conjectured identity <a href="#eq:kraetzer" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:kraetzer">[eq:kraetzer]</a> is false.

For every $f\in\mathcal S$ and $|z|=r<1$, $$\label{eq:koebe}
 \frac{1-r}{(1+r)^3}\le |f'(z)|\le
 \frac{1+r}{(1-r)^3},\qquad
 B(0,1/4)\subset f(\mathbb D).$$ If $F$ is univalent on the Euclidean disk $B(z_0,s)$, then $$\label{eq:local-quarter}
 B\bigl(F(z_0),s|F'(z_0)|/4\bigr)
 \subset F(B(z_0,s)).$$

The locally uniform topology makes $\mathcal K$ compact metrizable. Evaluation of $F,Q_F$ and all their derivatives is jointly continuous in $F,z$, and uniformly bounded on each compact source set. Moreover, uniformly in $F\in\mathcal K$, $$\label{eq:strip}
 |Q_F(x+iy)|\le C/y\quad(|x|\le2,\ 0<y\le1),
 \qquad |F(z)|\ge c>0\quad(|m(z)|\ge1/2).$$

Assume $\beta=\log_2\rho\ge1/4$, where $\rho$ is defined in <a href="#eq:rho" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:rho">[eq:rho]</a>. There is a Borel probability $P$ on $\mathcal K$ such that, writing $\mathbb E$ for expectation under $P$, $$\label{eq:law}
 \mathbb E\bigl[|Q_F(x+iy)|\,\Phi(T_{x+iy}F)\bigr]
 =y^{-\beta}\mathbb E\Phi(F)$$ for every $x+iy\in\mathbb H$ and every nonnegative Borel test $\Phi:\mathcal K\to[0,\infty]$.

The law satisfies $$\label{eq:moments}
 \mathbb Ea_F(i)=-\beta,\qquad
 \mathbb E|q_F(i)|^2=\beta(\beta+1).$$

If $I(F)<\infty$, then $$\label{eq:infinity}
 \frac{y^k}{|F(x+iy)|}\longrightarrow0
 \qquad\text{as } |x|+y\longrightarrow\infty.$$ For every compact target set $K$, its preimage restricted to $y\ge\epsilon>0$ is compact, its whole preimage has bounded height, and $$\label{eq:horizontal}
 \sup_{s>0}\int_\mathbb R\mathbf 1_K(F(x+is))\,dx<\infty.$$

For a good pair and $h>0$, $$\label{eq:count}
 \#\{z:\ z\text{ is a finite maximum},\ V_\xi^F(z)\ge h\}
 \ \ge\
 \#\{z:\ z\text{ is a saddle},\ V_\xi^F(z)\ge h\}.$$ Both counts are finite.

The positive-Jacobian mass at the base point is supported on good fibers: $$\label{eq:base-good}
 \mathbb E\bigl[J_{F,+}(i)
       \mathbf 1_{\{(F,G_F(i))\text{ not good}\}}\bigr]=0.$$

Let $g\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$ and $\beta\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$. As $T\to\infty$, uniformly for all $u\in\mathbb R$, $$\label{eq:quadratic-formula}
 \int_\mathbb Rg(y)\mathrm e(T\beta y^2/2-uy)\,dy
 =\frac{\mathrm e(\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}(\beta)/8-u^2/(2T\beta))}{\sqrt{T|\beta|}}
   \left[g\left(\frac{u}{T\beta}\right)+O_{g,\beta}(T^{-1})\right].$$

Let $I$ be a compact interval, let $\phi$ be smooth on a neighborhood of $I$ with $\phi''$ nowhere zero there, and let $f\in C_c^\infty(\operatorname{int}I)$. Uniformly for $x\in[0,1]$, $$\label{eq:stationary-phase}
 \sqrt N\int_I f(t)\mathrm e\bigl(N(\phi(t)-xt)\bigr)\,dt
 =\frac{f(t_x)}{\sqrt{|\phi''(t_x)|}}
   \mathrm e\bigl(N(\phi(t_x)-xt_x)+\mathop{\mathrm{sgn}}(\phi'')/8\bigr)+o(1),$$ where $t_x$ is the unique point with $\phi'(t_x)=x$ if it exists in $I$, and the right-hand main term is zero otherwise.

Let $I$ be a compact interval, $f\in C^1(I)$, and $\phi$ be real and smooth. If $\phi''=\Lambda\ne0$ on $I$, then for all real $k$, $$\label{eq:quadratic-bound}
 \left|\int_I f(t)\mathrm e(N\phi(t)-kt)\,dt\right|
 \le\frac{C}{\sqrt{N|\Lambda|}}
       \left(\lVert f\rVert_\infty+\int_I|f'(t)|\,dt\right).$$ If $N\phi'-k$ is monotone and has absolute value at least $N\rho>0$, the same bound holds with $C/(N\rho)$ in place of $C/\sqrt{N|\Lambda|}$.

Let $n\ge1$ be an integer and $Z_0,\ldots,Z_{n-1}\in\mathbb C$ satisfy $|Z_j|\le1$. Write $Z_j=d_j|Z_j|$, where $|d_j|=1$, choosing $d_j=1$ when $Z_j=0$, and put $$\mu=\frac12\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(1-|Z_j|).$$ There are $\eta_j\in\{d_j,-d_j\}$ such that $$\label{eq:complex-rounding}
 \sup_{t\in\mathbb T}\left|\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(\eta_j-Z_j)\mathrm e(jt)\right|
 \le C\left(1+\sqrt{\mu\log(80n/\mu)}\right),$$ with an absolute constant $C$. The square-root term is zero for $\mu=0$, when the error can be zero. For real inputs the outputs are real signs. The same estimate holds on any interval of $n$ consecutive integer frequencies.
